#{[17592186409898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409898 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "38cdacac-1881-5ec8-b9e9-d206d7013904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409899 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/592dc9d0d4713406cf9bf9c6ad966cb4446420113aa0e94fcfdcdb35b6471a9c.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409900 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1045 "Describing Planar Regions Using Set-Builder Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186420381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420380 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e43a9c22-1d1e-5d1e-868e-d443424ae7b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420381 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420381 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e86ea9a-9531-5eb8-9292-ae72f3485efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420381 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186287721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420382 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186